 "chapter": "7",
 "title": "Lewis Symbols and Structures",
 "short": "Lewis Symbols and Structures",
 "lead": "A Lewis symbol shows the valence electrons of an atom or a monatomic ion as dots around its symbol, and a Lewis structure shows how the valence electrons of a molecule or polyatomic ion are shared in bonds or held as lone pairs. This section uses the octet rule to predict how many bonds an atom forms, sets out a five-step procedure for writing Lewis structures, including those with double and triple bonds, and describes the molecules whose central atoms cannot have eight electrons.",
 "objectives": [
  "Write Lewis symbols for neutral atoms and ions",
  "Draw Lewis structures depicting the bonding in simple molecules"
 ],
 "summary_html": "<p>Valence electronic structures can be visualized by drawing Lewis symbols (for atoms and monatomic ions) and Lewis structures (for molecules and polyatomic ions). Lone pairs, unpaired electrons, and single, double, or triple bonds are used to indicate where the valence electrons are located around each atom in a Lewis structure. Most structures—especially those containing second row elements—obey the octet rule, in which every atom (except H) is surrounded by eight electrons. Exceptions to the octet rule occur for odd-electron molecules (free radicals), electron-deficient molecules, and hypervalent molecules.</p>",
 "notes": "Nothing of the book’s section is left out. The Fullerene Chemistry note keeps the book’s stray “since prehistoric times, .” and the period inside “C<sub>60.</sub>”, and Example 7.5 keeps the book’s “six steps discussed earlier” for the five-step procedure.",
